      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Write a search title for each state. Prove that no matches, no search yet, and failed work have different titles.",
        "steps": [
          "Declare idle, loading, loaded(providers:), and failed(message:) in SearchState. Write title(for:) with no default branch.",
          "Print a title for each of the four cases. Also print the title for loaded with an empty array.",
          "Use if case to extract names from one loaded result. Explain where the new names binding can be used.",
          "Temporarily add offline. Record the missing-branch error you see, or predict it if you cannot run the compiler.",
          "Decide whether offline needs its own case for your product. Explain retry, retained content, and what assistive technology should announce."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Every declared state has explicit rendering behavior.",
          "An empty loaded array produces no matches rather than start search or retry.",
          "The new offline case exposes a required switch update.",
          "Unknown raw strings are handled without force unwrapping.",
          "Your explanation distinguishes representation from transition rules."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>A renderer turns a state into something the user can read. The lesson's title function is one worked renderer. Put these calls after SearchState and title in the same standalone file. Predict the printed lines first, then compare them with the answer.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">let samples: [SearchState] = [\n    .idle,\n    .loading,\n    .loaded(providers: [\"Dr. Ada\"]),\n    .loaded(providers: []),\n    .failed(message: \"Retry when connected\")\n]\nfor sample in samples {\n    print(title(for: sample))\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The expected lines are Start a search, Loading, 1 providers, No matches, and Error: Retry when connected. The teaching string intentionally leaves pluralization simple; real localized UI would address that separately. Empty success never takes the failure branch.\u003c/p>\u003cp>After adding offline, extend the switch with \u003ccode>case .offline: return \"Offline: retry later\"\u003c/code>. A separate case is justified if it drives a distinct affordance, such as retaining stale content or displaying a connection explanation. If all failures share the same behavior, a typed failure payload can be enough instead. Neither decision is universally correct; make the user-facing rule explicit.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The compiler protects coverage, but it cannot decide whether a retry should be enabled or whether cached names should remain visible. Those are transition and presentation requirements. For a raw code, preserve nil from unknown input until the boundary deliberately maps it to a fallback. Record the added branch and product rationale as your exercise evidence rather than treating this solution as completion.\u003c/p>"
      },
