      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Build the counter in a separate scratch iOS app. Explain what happens when another screen covers it, you return, and you finally release it.",
        "steps": [
          "Create CounterController from the example and show it in a UINavigationController.",
          "Add breakpoint actions for viewDidLoad, viewDidAppear, viewDidDisappear, and deinit. Keep event notes separate from count changes.",
          "Tap twice, push a blank detail controller, then pop back. Explain why the counter still shows two.",
          "Draw navigation controller → counter → view tree → button → action. Label the weak capture back to the counter.",
          "Add a small hosting controller with the full child setup sequence. Then remove its view and controller relationship correctly."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The counter remains two after returning from detail.",
          "Constraints adapt to the container's safe area without duplicate installation.",
          "The ownership drawing explains why disappearing does not imply deallocation.",
          "The child controller receives containment completion and is removable without orphaning its view."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The navigation stack keeps the same counter controller alive while the detail covers it. Its count property therefore stays two when you return. Setup should not build a second hierarchy or reset the count on each appearance. Your log may show one initial load and several appearance callbacks. After popping the counter itself, clear any scratch references that also own it before expecting deinit.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The action has a weak reference to the counter; the button and action therefore do not keep the controller alive through a strong reference cycle. A temporarily promoted \u003ccode>self\u003c/code> remains alive during the synchronous event, which is what the render requires. The parent retains its hosted child through containment. To remove that child, use the following sequence in a main-actor UI context:\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">child.willMove(toParent: nil)\nchild.view.removeFromSuperview()\nchild.removeFromParent()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Here \u003ccode>child\u003c/code> is the hosted controller retained by your scratch owner. If you retain it in a separate property, clear that property as part of the owner's removal policy. Do not force a deallocation conclusion from a visibility log: first audit every remaining strong reference. The worked answer is a design and inspection guide; this course author has not built or run your scratch app.\u003c/p>"
      },
