      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Own one observable draft in a scratch SwiftUI screen. Add an editor and summary, then reset it by deliberately changing identity.",
        "steps": [
          "In a separate iOS 17+ target, write DemoDraft, DraftScreen, and DraftEditor from the example.",
          "Add a summary child with a plain DemoDraft property. Read name and canContinue in its body.",
          "Type a name, toggle the reminder, and change unrelated parent state. Explain why the same draft remains.",
          "Give the parent @State resetKey = 0. Apply .id(resetKey) to DraftScreen and increment resetKey from a Reset button.",
          "Compare the address and member-ID wrappers. For each, identify who keeps the model alive and whether any data is persisted."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The editor and summary share one model reference and respond to name edits.",
          "Unrelated parent updates preserve the draft while identity is stable.",
          "A deliberate identity change creates fresh draft state.",
          "The explanation explicitly separates observation, model ownership, and persistence."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>A read-only child needs no binding wrapper. Reading observable properties in its body lets SwiftUI track those dependencies. It receives the same model reference as the editor. The screen that created the model keeps it in State while its identity stays the same.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">@MainActor\nstruct DraftSummary: View {\n    let draft: DemoDraft\n    var body: some View {\n        Text(draft.canContinue ? draft.name : \"Incomplete\")\n    }\n}\n\n@MainActor\nstruct ResettableDraft: View {\n    @State private var resetKey = 0\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack {\n            DraftScreen().id(resetKey)\n            Button(\"Reset draft\") { resetKey += 1 }\n        }\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Add \u003ccode>DraftSummary(draft: draft)\u003c/code> beside the editor in \u003ccode>DraftScreen\u003c/code>. The parent key is an \u003ccode>Int\u003c/code> inferred from zero; changing it deliberately replaces the child identity. You should observe a new empty name and false reminder after Reset. This behavior is a product choice, so a production app should consider unsaved-edit confirmation separately. Both demo models here are in-memory; no wrapper promises disk restoration. The address model uses Observation plus State ownership, while the member-ID consumer observes an externally supplied ObservableObject. The latter boundary must be traced to its supplying owner rather than inferred from the word Observed.\u003c/p>"
      },
