      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Use the synthetic loading screen to show success, failure, a replacement request, and cancellation. Write the visible-state rule first.",
        "steps": [
          "Create the shown service, model, and screen in a separate iOS 17+ target.",
          "Pass demo-1 to show success. Pass fail to show the made-up unavailable error.",
          "Add parent buttons that switch between two IDs quickly. Record which request is allowed to update the screen.",
          "Leave the screen during its delay, then return. Explain whether this new appearance should start another load.",
          "Write the order of events for two overlapping calls. Decide what should happen to an old success and an old failure.",
          "Read the member-ID reset and check after fetching. Compare its manual-entry rule with this example's cancellation-to-idle rule."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Success and failure have explicit, mutually exclusive rendering states.",
          "Cancelled latest work does not become a user-facing unavailable error.",
          "An older request cannot overwrite or clear a newer request's phase.",
          "The learner can identify which Task is associated with the view and which is manually owned.",
          "No real network or patient data is used."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Request A starts with revision 1. While A waits, B starts with revision 2. If A later succeeds, its revision is old, so it returns without changing state. The same check in catch stops an old failure or cancellation from clearing B's state. B alone may publish loaded, failed, or idle under this model's chosen rules.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For deterministic model reasoning, replace the delay with a controlled fake in your own scratch target so you can complete B before A. A direct service dependency makes this easy: inject a main-actor async throwing closure or protocol rather than depending on wall-clock timing. Do not claim the rapid-tap manual experiment proves all schedules; it only demonstrates one observed run.\u003c/p>\u003cp>When the current screen's task is cancelled, the model checks the task flag or CancellationError and uses its chosen idle policy. A production form could preserve prior data instead. The member-ID feature specifically resets to manual entry because that is the user's action, and its post-fetch cancellation check protects that decision. The important result is a documented transition policy plus a freshness guard, not a particular spinner or timeout duration.\u003c/p>"
      },
