      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Build the five-checkpoint draft flow in a separate iOS app. Record its behavior and owners, using the client only as a reference.",
        "steps": [
          "Write the acceptance rules before reading the answer. Choose an iOS 17+ scratch target.",
          "Create one observable draft with displayName, an optional reason, calculated canReview, and reset().",
          "Build root editing, a modal picker with Cancel, and a pushed Review screen. Pass the same draft to each.",
          "Reuse UIKitMessage and DemoLoadingScreen from lessons 010 and 011. Use made-up input and give the message row an explicit size.",
          "Add model tests for spaces-only text, missing selection, valid draft, and Reset. Check modal Cancel and returning from Review in the app.",
          "Record the input sequence, checks actually run, ownership, and persistence limits. Label unrun checks as planned."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "A whitespace-only name cannot open Review.",
          "Selection updates the shared draft; Cancel preserves an existing choice.",
          "Returning from Review preserves the root draft.",
          "Reset clears name and reason without implicitly replacing navigation.",
          "The UIKit bridge updates its existing controller from current input.",
          "Review's asynchronous work has a view-associated lifetime and a synthetic failure fixture.",
          "The verification note distinguishes executed checks from planned checks."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Try your implementation before using this reference. Put these types in one scratch iOS 17+ module. Include UIKitMessage, DemoLoader, DemoLoadingModel, and DemoLoadingScreen from lessons 010–011. Foundation provides trimming, Observation provides the macro, and SwiftUI provides the controls. The code shows one design; it does not complete your learner task.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">import Foundation\nimport Observation\nimport SwiftUI\n\nenum PracticeReason: String, CaseIterable, Identifiable {\n    case chat = \"Demo chat\"\n    case checkIn = \"Demo check-in\"\n    var id: Self { self }\n}\n\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class PracticeDraft {\n    var displayName = \"\"\n    var selectedReason: PracticeReason?\n\n    var canReview: Bool {\n        !displayName.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines).isEmpty\n            &amp;&amp; selectedReason != nil\n    }\n\n    func reset() {\n        displayName = \"\"\n        selectedReason = nil\n    }\n}\n\n@MainActor\nstruct PracticeRoot: View {\n    @State private var draft = PracticeDraft()\n    @State private var showsPicker = false\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            Form {\n                Section(\"Synthetic input\") {\n                    TextField(\"Demo display name\", text: $draft.displayName)\n                    Button(draft.selectedReason?.rawValue ?? \"Choose demo reason\") {\n                        showsPicker = true\n                    }\n                }\n                Section {\n                    NavigationLink(\"Review\") {\n                        PracticeReview(draft: draft)\n                    }\n                    .disabled(!draft.canReview)\n                    Button(\"Reset\") { draft.reset() }\n                }\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Practice draft\")\n            .sheet(isPresented: $showsPicker) {\n                PracticePicker(draft: draft)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n@MainActor\nstruct PracticePicker: View {\n    @Environment(\\.dismiss) private var dismiss\n    let draft: PracticeDraft\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List(PracticeReason.allCases) { reason in\n                Button(reason.rawValue) {\n                    draft.selectedReason = reason\n                    dismiss()\n                }\n            }\n            .navigationTitle(\"Demo reason\")\n            .toolbar {\n                Button(\"Cancel\") { dismiss() }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n@MainActor\nstruct PracticeReview: View {\n    let draft: PracticeDraft\n    var body: some View {\n        Form {\n            Text(draft.selectedReason?.rawValue ?? \"No selection\")\n            UIKitMessage(message: \"Demo name: \\(draft.displayName)\")\n                .frame(height: 100)\n            DemoLoadingScreen(id: draft.selectedReason?.rawValue ?? \"demo-1\")\n        }\n        .navigationTitle(\"Review\")\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The Boolean conjunction \u003ccode>&amp;&amp;\u003c/code> requires both validity conditions. Root State retains the draft; the picker writes that same object through its plain reference. Bindable is unnecessary here because its buttons assign a property directly rather than requiring a Binding projection. Its Cancel action only dismisses, so an existing choice remains. Review uses ordinary observable reads and a dynamic UIKit message. The modal's separate stack supplies its own toolbar scope. Reset mutates the draft explicitly; it does not use a random ID or replace the stack.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the failure checkpoint, temporarily pass the literal \u003ccode>\"fail\"\u003c/code> to DemoLoadingScreen in your own scratch version, then restore the reason-based input. A model test should assign a whitespace name and a reason and expect false, assign Demo Ada and expect true, call reset and expect empty name, nil reason, and false. The worked code is a reference design inspected for consistency; it is not a claim that an app binary or screenshot was produced.\u003c/p>"
      },
