      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Build a scratch read client for made-up cards using a fake transport. Check status, decoding, and cancellation separately. Add no retries until you have written an endpoint retry rule.",
        "steps": [
          "Define a Sendable transport protocol that returns bytes and response information.",
          "Require an HTTP response and check this endpoint's success statuses before decoding.",
          "Convert the response DTO into a small domain value. Preserve an absent subtitle as nil.",
          "Use fakes for valid JSON, 401, 204, malformed JSON, and cancellation. Also check the four NoteChange fixtures for missing, null, string, and wrong type."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Non-HTTP responses and unacceptable statuses cannot become valid cards.",
          "Required fields fail if absent or malformed; optional subtitle permits missing and null.",
          "Cancellation propagates without retry or empty success.",
          "All fixtures and identifiers are synthetic; no real service is contacted."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The Foundation client below receives its transport from the caller. A fake can supply response bytes without a server. UI code never needs to decode them, and the client never picks a global URLSession. This endpoint expects a JSON body on success. A \u003ccode>204\u003c/code> means no content, so this particular client rejects it rather than trying to decode an absent card.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">import Foundation\n\nprotocol CardTransport: Sendable {\n    func data(for request: URLRequest) async throws -&gt; (Data, URLResponse)\n}\nstruct Card: Sendable, Equatable {\n    let id: String\n    let title: String\n    let subtitle: String?\n}\nenum CardReadError: Error {\n    case nonHTTP\n    case status(Int)\n    case emptyRepresentation\n}\nstruct CardClient: Sendable {\n    let transport: any CardTransport\n\n    func read(_ request: URLRequest) async throws -&gt; Card {\n        try Task.checkCancellation()\n        let (data, response) = try await transport.data(for: request)\n        try Task.checkCancellation()\n        guard let http = response as? HTTPURLResponse else {\n            throw CardReadError.nonHTTP\n        }\n        guard (200...299).contains(http.statusCode) else {\n            throw CardReadError.status(http.statusCode)\n        }\n        guard http.statusCode != 204, !data.isEmpty else {\n            throw CardReadError.emptyRepresentation\n        }\n        let dto = try JSONDecoder().decode(CardDTO.self, from: data)\n        return Card(id: dto.id, title: dto.title, subtitle: dto.subtitle)\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>\u003ccode>any CardTransport\u003c/code> stores an existential dependency: a value conforming to the protocol whose concrete type can vary. The protocol's Sendable requirement makes that dependency safe to carry with this client across isolation domains. The local decoder avoids concurrently mutating shared decoder configuration.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A fixture with status 401 and malformed bytes must yield the status error, proving HTTP validation precedes decoding. A 200 with a numeric title must yield a decoding error. A transport throwing cancellation must keep that cancellation observable. Use \u003ccode>https://example.invalid/cards/synthetic-1\u003c/code> only to construct request and response metadata for a fake; do not send it over the network.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This minimal client deliberately has no refresh or retry implementation. Its worked boundary makes those policies testable additions rather than accidental generic behavior. In a production adapter, inject the repository's approved transport, define response size and content-type constraints, and put account refresh policy in its established owner.\u003c/p>"
      },
