      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Review the checkout's request boundary without editing it. In a scratch target, make a route gateway for made-up reads. Propose one small change with behavior checks and recovery rules.",
        "steps": [
          "Trace SearchServiceModule, request processing, credential ownership, alternate dispatcher construction, and startup selection.",
          "At each boundary, record state ownership, lifetime, failure, account scope, and the source that supports your claim.",
          "Make a gateway that captures its selected implementation before awaiting a made-up read.",
          "Pause one fake read, switch selection, and release it. Check that only later reads use the new route.",
          "Write the review with fixture coverage, intended differences, stop conditions, and production rollout facts you have not verified."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Every claim about the checkout has a verified active file anchor.",
          "The gateway preserves an in-flight request's chosen implementation.",
          "The review distinguishes route snapshotting from draining and rollback from replay.",
          "No existing client source, flag, credential, or live request is modified.",
          "The proposal has a concrete scope and caller-visible acceptance criteria."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>This scratch gateway keeps the selected route stable for one read. Its actor owns selection; its two readers follow one Sendable protocol. The method saves the chosen dependency before it waits. Another call to \u003ccode>select\u003c/code> can change future selection during that wait, but cannot change this method's saved dependency. The example proves route consistency for synthetic reads, not the checkout's full migration mechanism.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">struct MigrationCard: Sendable, Equatable {\n    let id: String\n    let title: String\n}\nprotocol MigrationReading: Sendable {\n    func read(id: String) async throws -&gt; MigrationCard\n}\nenum MigrationRoute: Sendable { case existing, alternate }\n\nactor MigrationGateway {\n    private var route: MigrationRoute = .existing\n    private let existing: any MigrationReading\n    private let alternate: any MigrationReading\n\n    init(\n        existing: any MigrationReading,\n        alternate: any MigrationReading\n    ) {\n        self.existing = existing\n        self.alternate = alternate\n    }\n\n    func select(_ newRoute: MigrationRoute) {\n        route = newRoute\n    }\n\n    func read(id: String) async throws -&gt; MigrationCard {\n        try Task.checkCancellation()\n        let selected: any MigrationReading\n        switch route {\n        case .existing: selected = existing\n        case .alternate: selected = alternate\n        }\n        let result = try await selected.read(id: id)\n        try Task.checkCancellation()\n        return result\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>A worked review would state: “Observed: app composition creates an alternate configured URLSession dispatcher, and startup can enable it. Proposed scope: test one read operation through both injectable providers with the same synthetic fixtures. Preserve status validation, cancellation, credential adaptation, interception configuration, and tracing. Do not change account ownership or replay failed writes. Rollback selects the existing implementation for future operations. Runtime flag exposure and device performance remain unverified.”\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the route test, start a read against an existing fake and hold it, select alternate, then release the existing fake. Its result must still come from existing. Start a second read and verify alternate receives it. If draining is required, add explicit active-operation tracking and tests for when promotion happens rather than renaming this gateway.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The learning gateway does not reproduce the real client's interception, retries, draining, or tracing. It isolates one correctness property so you can reason about it. A production proposal should attach the exact operation's fixture parity results and identify the composition owner before a rollout. This is a review artifact and synthetic experiment, not a completed migration.\u003c/p>"
      },
