      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Write an incident note for a made-up build with three cases: a trap, a memory-pressure termination, and a slow request that does not terminate the app. Keep their evidence paths separate.",
        "steps": [
          "Define eligible attempts, confirmed success, expected cancellation, and build/OS groups.",
          "Choose a diagnostic artifact for each case and list what you need before claiming a cause.",
          "Allow only fixed safe state and failure fields in your proposed event, rather than copying request payloads.",
          "Write containment actions and say which installed clients each can actually affect.",
          "Choose one hypothesis. Plan a regression test for it and a recovery check after release."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The artifact does not treat all three cases as ordinary crashes",
          "Symbol retention includes binary UUID matching",
          "The outcome metric has an explicit denominator",
          "Containment has an owner and an affected-client boundary",
          "Hypotheses, observations, and synthetic numbers remain distinguishable"
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>This worked note counts a submission only after an authenticated person confirms it and the client accepts the attempt. Retries use the same approved logical-operation identity so they do not inflate the count. Navigation cancellation before submission is separate. A timeout afterward stays unresolved until reconciliation checks what happened. Success requires receiving and presenting the confirmed state; an HTTP success alone is not enough.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Case A has a symbolicated trap report for synthetic build 410. The matching dSYM UUID is verified; a mapper frame suggests a stale-index hypothesis. The proposed regression test replaces the result array before a delayed selection callback and verifies lookup by stable ID. That test supports the mechanism only after reproducing the failure in the candidate code. Case B has a jetsam report without an application backtrace: investigate repeated image retention and device memory pressure with allocation and ownership evidence. Case C stays alive but times out: follow its trace and reconcile server state before retrying.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The schema allows app/build, coarse OS cohort, operation state, bounded outcome code, and duration bucket. It excludes names, appointment details, tokens, and response bodies. Any operation identifier must receive its own access and retention review rather than being assumed anonymous.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Containment owner: release captain for rollout pause, feature owner for compatible flag disablement, service owner for reconciliation. Pausing does not downgrade build 410, and a remote flag cannot protect a path reached before flag retrieval. The recovery check compares confirmed-success rate and trap incidence within the same build/OS cohort after the fix, with enough attempts and an allowance for reporting delay. All incident data here is synthetic; your report must attach actual artifacts before claiming a diagnosed production defect.\u003c/p>"
      },
