        "solution": "\u003cp>Keep the account service and data repository at process scope so scenes can share them deliberately. Each scene root owns its own route, sheet, focus, and selection. Give a saved draft a domain ID and load it from the correct account repository. If two windows share that draft live, define how edits and conflicting commits are handled. If they edit independently, give each its own temporary buffer and explicit commit. Scene navigation and durable draft storage remain separate responsibilities.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A minimal restoration envelope can encode version 1, account-scope marker, draft ID, and an optional selected item ID. On restoration, first verify version and current account, then load the draft and resolve the selected item. Fall back to the list when the selected item no longer exists. Never store credentials in this envelope, and never treat decoding a route as authority to resubmit a request. SceneStorage can keep small UI selections while the repository protects the draft.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The checkout comparison should name production's app-delegate window construction and the demo's WindowGroup/scene manifest, iPhone/iPad configured destinations, production's portrait-phone/four-orientation-tablet plist, production's separate UIDesignRequiresCompatibility declaration, and QA's UIRequiresFullScreen = false. Add Apple's current SDK-linked scene adoption requirement as a separate verified policy. These facts identify what needs a release check; they do not show that a production launch or two-window test passed. Restoration and actual multiwindow interaction remain learner/specialist runtime work.\u003c/p>"
