      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Design a mapping benchmark and a separate search-to-usable-results experiment. Calculate percentiles from synthetic values and label them as examples.",
        "steps": [
          "Write the operation, fixture, environment, and cache rules before running. State which outcomes are counted in each failure or latency rate.",
          "Implement nearest-rank percentiles. Check empty input, one value, and twenty values.",
          "Draw a mapping interval inside the longer UI operation. Include end paths for failure and cancellation.",
          "Prepare a report with raw samples, count, median, p95, range, conditions, and device questions you have not yet answered."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Nearest-rank p95 of integers 1 through 20 is 19; this is arithmetic evidence only.",
          "Empty input returns absence and invalid percentile requests have a stated precondition.",
          "Every before/after comparison uses the same fixture and declared reset policy.",
          "Physical-device confirmation and measurement status are explicit; blank observations are never replaced with invented timings."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The standalone helper below calculates a percentile from finite, nonnegative samples, for example durations in milliseconds. Finite means the value is neither infinity nor an invalid numeric value. Validate these requirements at the harness boundary, the test code that supplies input and checks results. Keep the original samples before sorting so you retain their order and context. This helper implements nearest-rank arithmetic. It does not calculate statistical confidence or turn synthetic values into device observations.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">func nearestRank(_ samples: [Double], percentile p: Double) -&gt; Double? {\n    precondition(p &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; p &lt;= 1)\n    precondition(samples.allSatisfy { $0.isFinite &amp;&amp; $0 &gt;= 0 })\n    guard !samples.isEmpty else { return nil }\n    let ordered = samples.sorted()\n    let rank = Int((p * Double(ordered.count)).rounded(.up))\n    return ordered[max(1, rank) - 1]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The external second label is \u003ccode>percentile:\u003c/code>, while \u003ccode>p\u003c/code> is the internal name. \u003ccode>Double?\u003c/code> permits absence for an empty dataset. \u003ccode>guard\u003c/code> exits early. Rounding upward implements the rank definition, and subtracting one converts it to an array index. For \u003ccode>(1...20).map(Double.init)\u003c/code>, p95 is nineteen. Do not use this helper's p50 as an interpolated even-sample median without explaining the distinction: nearest-rank p50 selects the tenth of twenty values, while a conventional sample median averages the tenth and eleventh.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The worked report has columns for revision, physical device, OS, configuration, fixture, reset policy, sample index, outcome, total milliseconds, mapping milliseconds, and thermal notes. Leave timing fields unmeasured. State: “Mapping will be diagnosed with a signpost and Time Profiler; responsiveness will be confirmed with an actual interaction on the target phone.” Baseline/candidate order alternates by batch, and all errors remain counted separately. Attach raw records and matching trace/result artifacts after execution. This separates a tested arithmetic helper from a still-pending performance claim.\u003c/p>"
      },
