      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Use synthetic images in a scratch Apple-platform target to compare full loading and thumbnails. Plan separate checks for array copying and object lifetime.",
        "steps": [
          "Declare source dimensions, orientation, display points and scale, compressed-byte limit, and output pixel bound.",
          "Check the first-frame, aspect ratio, and orientation policy for valid, corrupt, and extreme-aspect images.",
          "Plan matching allocation and footprint runs with one decode and several bounded decodes, then repeated screen entry and exit.",
          "Keep both array values observable after a COW mutation. Also inspect a small slice that retains a large source buffer."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "For the thumbnail path, the output longest dimension respects the chosen bound and orientation is correct.",
          "Estimated pixel arithmetic is labeled as estimated, with actual footprint fields left pending until measurement.",
          "Repeated route exits have an ownership explanation for remaining objects and caches.",
          "No pool encloses an await or is claimed to drain future callbacks; invalid source errors remain distinguishable."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>An 80-point square at scale three starts with a 240-pixel square display requirement. A square source fits the function's 240-pixel longest-edge bound directly. A wide panorama may need a different decode-and-crop plan to fill that square sharply; do not stretch it silently. ImageIO applies orientation metadata and returns a CGImage. A UIKit adapter can create a UIImage with the intended scale under its UI ownership and isolation rules. Check output appearance separately from the theoretical pixel budget.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Measure compressed bytes, source dimensions, output dimensions, decoded buffer estimate, transient peak, and retained population independently. Run the baseline and thumbnail candidate with the same source and output appearance. Keep fixture creation out of a decode-only interval. The candidate may reduce decoded output storage, but do not claim a peak reduction until the allocation/footprint evidence exists. Multiple overlapping decodes and any retained full-size intermediary can change the conclusion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For COW, retain both original and edited arrays, mutate the edited element, and verify original remains unchanged. Compare allocation behavior with a uniquely owned mutation under the same optimization settings. Then keep a tiny slice after releasing the original binding and inspect retention; compare with an explicit small Array copy. The worked conclusion separates proven value semantics from implementation-dependent allocation behavior. ARC ownership, temporary autorelease lifetime, and peak pixel storage remain three distinct explanations.\u003c/p>"
      },
