      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Extend lesson 015's bounded loader in a scratch harness. Define resource, cancellation, and cache rules, then compare limits with controlled fake work.",
        "steps": [
          "Use a positive active limit, indexed result slots, and cancellation checks before adding replacement work.",
          "Use an actor-isolated fake to record active operations and let you control their completion.",
          "Set byte limits and decide what a partial failure means. Name the owning service and whether one waiter's cancellation stops work shared by other waiters.",
          "Use fake low-power, thermal, and memory-pressure conditions. For each, define when to admit new work, stop active work, show a fallback, and recover through a bounded queue.",
          "Compare active limits 1, 2, and 4. Record completed work per unit of time, p95, peak bytes, and transfer counts. Keep energy fields pending until device observation."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Peak active calls never exceed the chosen limit, even when completions occur out of order.",
          "An empty input returns empty output and invalid limits fail explicitly.",
          "Cancellation/failure is never converted to successful partial output.",
          "Logout invalidation prevents old cache commits and an independent UI generation prevents stale publication.",
          "Optional work reacts to fake pressure changes without a restart storm or a false assumption that a warning always precedes termination.",
          "Energy fields require actual device/tool observations; request count alone is not labeled battery savings."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>This standalone Swift window limits how many child tasks are created at once. It returns String snapshots that satisfy Sendable, the contract for safe transfer across concurrency boundaries. That window does not limit final output size, requests per second, or bytes in each payload. Give those separate rules in the harness. Keep cancellation cooperative and preserve input order with parent-owned result slots. The code supplies a bounded-work design, not evidence that any particular limit is fastest or saves battery.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">enum WindowError: Error { case invalidLimit, incomplete }\n\nfunc windowed(\n    _ ids: [String], limit: Int,\n    load: @escaping @Sendable (String) async throws -&gt; String\n) async throws -&gt; [String] {\n    guard limit &gt; 0 else { throw WindowError.invalidLimit }\n    try Task.checkCancellation()\n    return try await withThrowingTaskGroup(of: (Int, String).self) { group in\n        var output = [String?](repeating: nil, count: ids.count)\n        var next = 0\n        for _ in 0..&lt;min(limit, ids.count) {\n            let index = next\n            let id = ids[index]\n            next += 1\n            group.addTask {\n                try Task.checkCancellation()\n                return (index, try await load(id))\n            }\n        }\n        while let (index, value) = try await group.next() {\n            try Task.checkCancellation()\n            output[index] = value\n            if next &lt; ids.count {\n                let index = next\n                let id = ids[index]\n                next += 1\n                group.addTask {\n                    try Task.checkCancellation()\n                    return (index, try await load(id))\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        try Task.checkCancellation()\n        return try output.map { value in\n            guard let value else { throw WindowError.incomplete }\n            return value\n        }\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>\u003ccode>@escaping\u003c/code> allows the loader to be retained by child closures; \u003ccode>@Sendable\u003c/code> constrains captures. Only the parent writes optional output slots. One completion frees one position in the active window before a replacement is added. The final map refuses a missing result instead of using compactMap to silently publish fewer items.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use gates to complete the third input first and prove input ordering. Cancel the outer task with children waiting, release/cancel fake gates, and verify no replacement or UI commit occurs after the relevant checks. A fake must cooperate or the structured scope will wait for it. Keep a service-owned generation for cache commits and a feature-owned generation for visible publication. Compare fixed workloads at each limit on the same device; do not infer an optimal number or energy improvement from this code alone.\u003c/p>"
      },
