      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Make a six-row table of library jobs, consumers, and review roles. Then test or paper-trace one small synthetic adapter without changing the client.",
        "steps": [
          "Choose six rows: transport, generated models, state or async work, UI, identity, and observability.",
          "For each row, record the requirement, resolution route, product, consuming target, job, and proposed review role.",
          "Compare the ReactiveKit source with the legacy guide wording. Find one retained PromiseKit bridge.",
          "Predict fixtures for entries, empty success, and offline failure. State that cancellation is not implemented by this fake.",
          "Write a narrow migration claim and list pending device, privacy, license, and human-ownership checks."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Every named active library has a manifest/product edge, with source imports recorded where inspected.",
          "The ledger does not confuse package names, modules, latest versions, or test-only inclusion.",
          "ReactiveKit and incomplete PromiseKit migration are described accurately.",
          "Empty success and offline failure stay distinct, and async is not described as automatic background execution.",
          "Ownership is recorded as confirmed evidence or a proposed reviewer role; no names or approvals are invented."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The example table connects each library name to a job and a consumer. Alamofire serves the ZDNetworking adapter. ApolloAPI supports generated schema modules. PromiseKit and ReactiveKit serve retained async contracts. SnapKit helps UIKit and Mezzanine code build constraints. Auth0 is declared by the authentication feature. OpenTelemetry and Sentry connect to tracing and error-related core targets. Include the checkout pin and resolution route in each row. These six rows illustrate the method; they do not cover the entire library inventory.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The CatalogFixture returns its entries for nonempty success, returns an empty successful array unchanged, and throws CatalogFailure.offline for failure. Its Result.get call preserves that distinction. It has no cancellation handler, live transport, resource lookup, or account policy. A test can check these three cases with local fixtures; passing them still leaves those missing requirements pending.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The migration statement can say that a chosen consumer would adopt a vendor-independent CatalogServing seam after behavior and lifecycle checks. It cannot say the client has migrated, because this lab changes no client source. Record the ZDRxCore naming mismatch and retained PromiseKit bridge as audit findings. Review roles are proposed until actual maintainers are confirmed. Device media, SDK data flow, selected-version licensing, and production startup measurements remain specialist or integration work.\u003c/p>"
      },
