        "solution": "\u003cp>A fresh counter starts at 0. One recordTap call changes it to 1, and the computed label then returns Taps: 1. If you run the scratch program, break on the actual source line inside recordTap. Check the selected frame, then inspect count before and after stepping over the increment. Evaluating label runs its getter. Calling recordTap through a debugger expression would add another tap and alter the experiment. These outputs remain predictions until you record a real stopped process.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the UIKit example, an owner must retain CourseTapTarget. Configure removes its external argument label, #selector(didTap(_:)) identifies a one-argument Objective-C method, and @objc exposes that method. MainActor requires appropriate UI access, while Objective-C exposure alone supplies no automatic thread hop. The active gesture source additionally stores a closure, whose captures need an independent ownership review.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A nil imported result suggests checking nullability and optional handling. An unrecognized selector suggests callback signature and Objective-C exposure. A missing generated Swift header suggests target/module identity and build membership. Mismatched release symbols require the actual binary's UUID-matching dSYMs. Host counter results do not validate UIKit delivery or a production crash; keep those observations pending unless executed in their proper targets.\u003c/p>"
