      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Build a fake refresh lease and a table of verification evidence. Control success, expiration, late results, and logout without registering pushes or editing client settings.",
        "steps": [
          "Use WorkFinishGate with a fake completion recorder and a Task handle kept by the owner.",
          "Give the store an account epoch and a may-commit flag. Keep validation and replacement in one serialized operation.",
          "Hold a fake response, expire the lease, then release that response even if it ignored cancellation.",
          "Repeat for success, failure, repeated finish calls, and an A-to-B-to-A account transition.",
          "List the APNs artifact checks separately and mark physical-device delivery pending."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Exactly one terminal completion is recorded per fake lease.",
          "Expiration cancels work and prevents a later response from replacing the snapshot.",
          "Changing account ownership blocks old writes even if the account text becomes A again.",
          "The ledger distinguishes source entitlements from signed archive evidence.",
          "No timing, APNs delivery, or background-transfer verification is claimed from the fake."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The scratch adapter needs two rules: report a terminal outcome once, and accept a durable update only from its current owner. Keep both decisions on MainActor for this experiment. Do not place an await between the ownership check and snapshot replacement. Use controlled fake gates from lesson 020 to choose which event arrives first. RefreshCommit below models only this acceptance boundary. It neither writes a database nor registers background work. Real disk commits need the same check inside their actual transaction owner.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">@MainActor\nfinal class RefreshCommit {\n    private var epoch = 0\n    private var accepting = true\n    private(set) var snapshot: [String] = []\n\n    func ticket() -&gt; Int { epoch }\n\n    func close() {\n        accepting = false\n        epoch += 1\n    }\n\n    func startNewOwner() {\n        epoch += 1\n        accepting = true\n        snapshot = []\n    }\n\n    func commit(_ values: [String], ticket: Int) -&gt; Bool {\n        guard accepting, ticket == epoch else { return false }\n        snapshot = values\n        return true\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Capture \u003ccode>ticket()\u003c/code> before awaiting the fake remote. On expiration call \u003ccode>close()\u003c/code>, cancel the retained task, and finish the gate as expired. When the fake is released, check Task cancellation, then call commit with the original ticket; it must fail even if the fake ignored cancellation. Normal success commits first and reports success only after that succeeds. Ordinary failure preserves the previous snapshot and reports failure. A second terminal call has no effect.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ccode>private(set)\u003c/code> lets the harness inspect the snapshot but keeps mutation inside the owner. Incrementing epoch invalidates all prior tickets. Starting another owner increments it again, so textual account equality cannot restore validity. The methods are synchronous; a disk adapter would need to perform the same validation in its actual transaction owner, not validate here and later await an unguarded file write.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The worked ledger records the fake's completion sequence, snapshot before and after each case, and pending device rows. The APNs row requests the installed bundle ID, signed aps environment, compatible profile/export path, provider endpoint and topic, current token binding, payload push type, and observed response. A foreground banner screenshot answers only presentation. No exact scheduler cadence or clinical reminder reliability follows from this exercise.\u003c/p>"
      },
