      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Make a synthetic Foundation fixture notebook for time, text, numbers, and files. Label host assertions separately from iOS device checks.",
        "steps": [
          "Construct Gregorian noon in America/New_York on 9 March 2024. Move one calendar day and compare elapsed seconds.",
          "Write the intended rule for a missing 02:30 and a repeated 01:30 before checking nextDate results.",
          "Compare composed and decomposed accents, including byte counts. Convert an emoji-adjacent range to NSRange and back.",
          "Parse decimal text with named locales, and keep the machine representation independent of displayed text.",
          "Plan missing, corrupt, locked, and failed-write cases without accessing client data."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The civil-day fixture reports 23 elapsed hours across the selected spring transition.",
          "Civil dates and floating reminder times are not silently represented as device-midnight instants.",
          "UTF-16 ranges convert through the exact string; out-of-bounds ranges reject, and character-based editing separately checks grapheme boundaries.",
          "Locale formatting never defines currency arithmetic or wire encoding.",
          "Protected-data, backup, and cross-process coordination remain explicitly unverified on a host."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The worked time fixture names the calendar and time zone rather than using changing device defaults. It constructs New York noon on 9 March 2024 and advances one calendar day. The expected local result is noon on 10 March; the expected duration is 82,800 seconds. Those assertions check different meanings. The Foundation code throws if constructing or advancing the fixture fails. Substituting the current Date would hide that failure and make the test’s inputs meaningless.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">enum FoundationFixtureError: Error { case invalidDate }\n\nfunc springDayFixture() throws -&gt; TimeInterval {\n    guard let zone = TimeZone(identifier: \"America/New_York\") else {\n        throw FoundationFixtureError.invalidDate\n    }\n    var calendar = Calendar(identifier: .gregorian)\n    calendar.timeZone = zone\n    guard let start = calendar.date(from: DateComponents(\n        year: 2024, month: 3, day: 9, hour: 12\n    )), let tomorrow = calendar.date(\n        byAdding: .day, value: 1, to: start\n    ) else {\n        throw FoundationFixtureError.invalidDate\n    }\n    let fields = calendar.dateComponents(\n        [.year, .month, .day, .hour], from: tomorrow\n    )\n    precondition(fields.day == 10 &amp;&amp; fields.hour == 12)\n    return tomorrow.timeIntervalSince(start)\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The expected interval for this named transition is 82,800 seconds. The code first verifies civil meaning, then measures elapsed time. A fixed 86,400-second addition would answer a different question. For the gap/repeat fixtures, write the expected adjustment or skip policy before running Calendar; do not choose policy by accepting whichever result arrives.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The worked text row observes one Character for the decomposed accent and more UTF-8 bytes than its composed counterpart, while equality is true. The emoji-adjacent selection round-trips through NSRange because the conversion uses the exact text. An out-of-bounds range must reject. A partial grapheme range may convert, so use isCharacterAligned to reject it for character-based editing instead of assuming the conversion performs that validation. Do not log real user text to diagnose it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The number row declares a visible locale and exact decimal or minor-unit contract. The file row treats missing content as empty only if that is the stated store contract, preserves corruption, and reports write failure without replacing it with success. Device rows request protection attributes, locked-open versus locked-reopen observations, and backup policy evidence. No file is created or learner exercise marked complete by this worked answer.\u003c/p>"
      },
