      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Write a distribution function with an optional pair result and a caption function with a default suffix.",
        "steps": [
          "Declare distribute(_ total: Int, among count: Int). Return an optional tuple with whole and remainder parts.",
          "Use guard to return nil for zero or negative counts.",
          "Try seven among two and seven among zero. Handle the optional result safely.",
          "Declare caption(for name: String, suffix: String = \"ready\"). Try both the default and an explicit suffix.",
          "Explain the external labels and every return path."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Seven/two returns whole three and remainder one.",
          "Invalid divisors return nil before arithmetic.",
          "The caption default and explicit suffix produce distinct expected strings.",
          "The solution uses no casts, force unwraps or client dependencies."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>This answer runs as a standalone file. It handles the tuple only after optional binding succeeds. The caption function returns text instead of printing it itself, so the caller controls output. Predict the five printed lines before running.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">func distribute(_ total: Int, among count: Int) -&gt; (whole: Int, remainder: Int)? {\n    guard count &gt; 0 else { return nil }\n    return (total / count, total % count)\n}\nfunc caption(for name: String, suffix: String = \"ready\") -&gt; String {\n    return \"\\(name): \\(suffix)\"\n}\nif let parts = distribute(7, among: 2) {\n    print(parts.whole)\n    print(parts.remainder)\n}\nprint(distribute(7, among: 0) == nil)\nprint(caption(for: \"Rae\"))\nprint(caption(for: \"Rae\", suffix: \"waiting\"))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Expected lines are 3, 1, true, Rae: ready and Rae: waiting. Tuple labels come from the declared return contract even though the return expression uses positional construction. The local name count is supplied under external among; total has no external label. Nil means this helper could not produce its result for the stated divisor, not that either component equals zero.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To test transfer, change the valid input to eight among three and predict two and two. Then state an additional domain rule, such as forbidding negative totals, and decide where to validate it. Do not assume positive divisor validation already covers that separate product requirement. Your own helper, predictions and observed run establish exercise evidence. These small synchronous operations do not establish service effects, actor isolation, or UI formatting correctness.\u003c/p>"
      },
