      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Count repeated labels and keep one copy of each label in first-appearance order.",
        "steps": [
          "Use the text labels general, dental, general, vision in that order.",
          "Count each label with a Dictionary default subscript.",
          "Use a seen Set and an output Array. Append a label only the first time it appears.",
          "Print counts by sorted key. Keep the unique list in input order.",
          "Show the difference between an absent key and an existing key whose count is zero."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "General count is two; dental and vision counts are one.",
          "Unique output is general, dental, vision in first-occurrence order.",
          "An absent key remains nil rather than silently becoming stored zero.",
          "The explanation states collision and presentation-order policies."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>This answer runs on its own and uses the syntax introduced so far. Set.insert returns a tuple; its inserted member tells you whether this label is new. That lets you append first occurrences without a second lookup. Predict the sorted count lines and the ordered unique list separately.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">let labels = [\"general\", \"dental\", \"general\", \"vision\"]\nvar counts: [String: Int] = [:]\nvar seen: Set&lt;String&gt; = []\nvar ordered: [String] = []\nfor label in labels {\n    counts[label, default: 0] += 1\n    if seen.insert(label).inserted {\n        ordered.append(label)\n    }\n}\nfor key in counts.keys.sorted() {\n    if let value = counts[key] { print(\"\\(key): \\(value)\") }\n}\nprint(ordered)\ncounts[\"empty\"] = 0\nprint(counts[\"absent\"] == nil)\nprint(counts[\"empty\"] == 0)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The histogram prints dental one, general two and vision one in sorted-key order. Ordered contains general, dental and vision in their first appearance order, independent of set traversal. Both final checks print true, while the dictionary states they test differ: absent has no association; empty has a present zero.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To test transfer, begin the input with vision and confirm that the unique output begins with vision while the sorted histogram still begins with dental. That checks the two ordering contracts independently. If duplicate labels should be rejected rather than counted, state a different rule before changing implementation. The present exercise validates made-up value behavior and does not establish production identifier correctness or benchmark hashing performance.\u003c/p>"
      },
