        "solution": "\u003cp>The timer's due time has arrived, but the loop must also check the right mode. Default mode excludes the timer registered in RuntimeLab.synthetic, so the count stays 0. Running the named custom mode handles the timer once and makes the count 1. This one-shot timer invalidates itself after delivery. The defer also provides cleanup if an earlier assertion throws. The Core Foundation calls each have a time limit, so a failed prediction becomes a reported host error rather than an intentionally hung process.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use one UIKit controller to own two timers and one DisplayDriver. Start them on MainActor when the screen enters the chosen active state. Invalidate both timers and call driver.stop when that state ends. Store only small synthetic counts and timestamp samples. Capture the controller weakly in a timer block, but still explicitly remove the registration. Build the separate scratch target before trying gestures. During a drag, compare both timer traces with the interaction. This comparison cannot promise a display cadence or reveal Combine's internal scheduling.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If every callback disappears while a synchronous decode occupies the main thread, adding a common-mode registration cannot free that thread. Give the expensive work a separate execution and ownership design. If only the default timer pauses while common-mode callbacks continue, the observations support a mode explanation. Keep the platform and observed order with your report. These are acceptance rules for interpreting your experiment, not claims that a UIKit run or learner exercise already succeeded.\u003c/p>"
