        "solution": "\u003cp>Keep the output array in the parent and make each child return its input index with its sum. Start two children. Await a completion, store the sum at that child's index, then start one replacement. This yields [7, 0, 5, 4] even if completion order differs. Before the first admissions and every replacement, check for a cancellation request. Once the parent observes one, stop adding work. If a child throws, preserve its error; the structured task-group scope cancels and joins remaining children instead of returning a successful-looking partial array.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For visible reads competing with prefetch, give one owner the admission decisions. Set an active limit of two, a named pending-item limit and a synthetic byte budget. Reserve an opportunity for visible work, or give it the next released slot. Replace older prefetches for the same identity rather than accumulating them. These numbers are design inputs to test, not device measurements or recommended optimums. Keep tokens for pending requests so a stop request can remove a waiting item and resume its caller with CancellationError exactly once.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Once a noninterruptible read starts, keep its resources valid until it returns. Record the cancellation request and reject or discard the eventual result according to your policy. Do not close a descriptor still in use. The supplied bridge instead waits for completion and checks cancellation afterward, a simpler policy for this fixture. Record backlog, retained bytes and foreground latency only in a workload experiment that actually measures them. Higher QoS and Task.detached do not define those limits. The earlier instructor checks support the mechanics; they do not complete your added fixture or establish a production rollout.\u003c/p>"
