        "solution": "\u003cp>First let a producer propose one integer. waitForPendingSend confirms it is registered. Calling next receives that integer; then join the producer. In the cancellation case, cancel the registered producer and join its CancellationError before offering a replacement value. The receiver should obtain only the replacement. Repeat from the receiver side: cancel and join the old waiter before sending to a new one. The two cases test different stored continuations, so passing one does not stand in for passing the other. A cancellation request must win the channel's state decision before it can remove that waiting operation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For time, call delayed with ManualClock and a five-second delay. Await waitForSleeper before advancing four seconds. Inspect pendingSleeps, advance one more second, then join the task and check ready. Cancel a later registered sleep, verify its waiter was removed, and advance past its old deadline. The helper removes registrations before resuming, preventing that old continuation from being resumed twice. For context, bind the synthetic label, join the child and Task results, and join the detached task's nil result. Check restoration after the nested binding and after the outer scope too.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For shutdown, call await channel.finish(), then join the retained task. Test a blocked sender and a blocked receiver separately. Also check repeated finish and rejection of new sends. If matching already won, a later cancellation request cannot retract the receipt. Completing the received work requires a separate bounded acknowledgment; sender.value alone cannot establish that. The host package supplies the local teaching implementation. Native lifecycle cleanup and integration with a pinned AsyncAlgorithms version remain separate verification tasks.\u003c/p>"
