      "exercise": {
        "brief": "In a scratch copy of UIRuntimeLab, replace CounterRecord and check that changes from the old record no longer reach the visible count.",
        "steps": [
          "Read DispatchLab.swift and CounterRecord.swift. Name who owns the record, token, and writes.",
          "Add a replace-model action. Invalidate the old token, store the new record, then register its callback.",
          "Keep the old record in a test fixture and change it after replacement. Check the current visible count.",
          "On a working iOS destination, record single and double taps, nil-target Reset, overlay pass-through, and leaving then returning.",
          "Run the macOS KVO check. Report its result separately from the UI observations."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The initial current-model value appears once per deliberate registration.",
          "Mutating the retired record never changes the visible current-model count.",
          "Single and double gestures never both commit for the same successful double tap.",
          "Returning to the screen catches the current value without duplicating registrations.",
          "The evidence record names the destination, exact actions, and unexecuted checks."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Make replacement one deliberate ownership change. Invalidate the old token before swapping the controller's strongly held CounterRecord. Register on the new record and store its token. Prepare the label before registering because .initial can call the closure before observe returns. Keep the retired record in the test fixture, change its count, and check that the visible count still belongs to the new record. Use [weak self] for the controller capture and keep all writers on the lab's main-thread contract.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The old fixture starts at zero. Registration produces zero immediately. Incrementing the current object to one produces one. Replace it with a new object whose count is ten; the new registration produces ten. Increment the old object to two and assert the display remains ten. Increment the new object to eleven and assert eleven. This sequence tests object replacement, whereas repeatedly mutating a single record only tests delivery. Keep those observations as separate assertions; a weak capture alone does not retire an old registration.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the gesture part, a successful double tap should run only doubleTap and add ten. A completed single tap should add one after the double recognizer has failed. Do not sleep for a guessed system recognition interval to declare the test passed; log which action actually fired. Reset should resolve to the containing controller's resetCount(_:) without a global target reference. Tapping through the empty overlay should still activate the actual button. Test VoiceOver activation of Increment and Reset separately because touch hit testing does not define accessibility focus.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The supplied host check already exercises initial/new delivery and idempotent token invalidation using the shared model on macOS. The replace-model UI extension and runtime interaction notebook remain learner work. If asynchronous callbacks are introduced, add a controlled stale-delivery fixture and reject an earlier registration generation on both success and error. Avoid presenting KVO cancellation as cancellation of arbitrary Task work. This solution supplies a precise ownership design and expected values, not a record that the learner completed it.\u003c/p>"
      },
