      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Add a Finish-at-destination button beside Freeze in the scratch lab. Record how each command changes the synthetic layer's state.",
        "steps": [
          "Build UIRuntimeLab using its README command and record the actual result.",
          "Add an action that removes travel and keeps the model endpoint.",
          "During a move, compare Finish with Freeze. Read the model and any available presentation sample.",
          "On a working destination, resize the canvas and turn on Reduce Motion.",
          "Record the latest command, model position, sample availability, and observed appearance separately."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Normal completion leaves the model at the latest destination.",
          "Freeze persists a pre-removal presentation sample when one is available.",
          "Finish removes travel and leaves the destination model unchanged.",
          "Resize has a documented clamp or proportional-position policy.",
          "Reduced Motion preserves endpoint semantics, and unexecuted frame measurements remain marked pending."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Finish keeps the destination already stored by move. Begin a transaction, disable implicit actions, remove travel, and commit without assigning marker.position. Wire the action to a normal UIButton target and selector in LayerController. Freeze instead writes its pre-removal presentation sample into the model. Both commands remove travel, but only Freeze changes that stored destination. A Task.sleep matching the animation duration cannot establish visual completion or handle interruption correctly.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a synthetic eighty-point start and two-hundred-point destination, suppose a presentation sample reports one hundred and twenty during the transition. Freeze should set the model to that sampled value and remove travel. Finish should leave the model at two hundred and remove travel. These numbers explain the two contracts; they are calculated hypothetical observations, not measurements from this run. Rapidly retargeting left then right should leave the latest endpoint in the model, with a new fromValue sampled before retiring the old animation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>During resizing, this lab first freezes and then clamps its center into the new fourteen-point endpoint margins. That is a geometry policy, not a promise that the same fractional progress survives. If fractional preservation is required, store semantic progress independently and recompute the model for the new width. Keep the radius and minimum-width behavior explicit. A content-size change can occur without a device-orientation event, so the view's actual bounds are the appropriate input.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Run on a functioning simulator or device before signing off the visual interaction. Inspect whether cancellation causes a jump, whether a nil presentation sample is explained, and whether leaving the tab retires visual work. With Reduce Motion enabled, the move action should immediately update the model and add no travel animation. The successful app build is useful API evidence; it does not certify these outcomes. Frame deadlines, real shimmer energy cost, rasterization tradeoffs, and GPU traces remain separate specialist measurements.\u003c/p>"
      },
