      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Add or independently reproduce bounded conflict resolution in the scratch outbox. Keep deletes, recreation, queue order, and accounts correct across restart.",
        "steps": [
          "Run HostChecks and both RestartProbe modes in fresh isolated directories. Record each actual result.",
          "Seed an accepted create, then queue a delete using the correct base revision. Reload before replay.",
          "Queue a stale edit and check that its conflict stops FIFO replay before later work is acknowledged.",
          "For resolveConflict, require one pending command for the record. Replace the conflict in its existing slot with a fresh ID. Reject dependent edits unless one explicit atomic policy reconciles them all.",
          "Switch A to B to A between send and acknowledgment. Record the remote effect separately from the rejected stale local commit."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Ordinary process restart reloads pending intent and stable operation identity.",
          "A lost response plus replay creates one synthetic server effect, including after receipt reload.",
          "Delete intent outlives the removed local row; stale updates cannot resurrect it automatically.",
          "Rebase has a fresh ID, preserves its queue position and commits queue/local state together; multiple pending edits to its record are rejected without changing bytes, and unrelated accounts remain unchanged.",
          "Disk/cancellation/conflict failures remain distinct and no device/background/production behavior is claimed."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Resolve one conflicted intent without moving it behind later work. The supplied reference validates cancellation and the account ticket, finds an entry marked conflict, and requires exactly one pending command for that record. It copies the journal, replaces the entry at its existing index with a fresh ID and supplied current revision, updates the copied optimistic record, and commits once. With two edits for that record, it throws dependentCommands before changing disk or memory. Removing and appending could let the later edit run first and advance the server revision. Supporting that case requires an explicit atomic reconciliation of every dependent intent and precondition.\u003c/p>\u003cp>HostChecks.rebaseChecks demonstrates both schedules. One conflicted edit is replaced in its original slot, its fresh ID produces one additional server effect, and its original receipt remains conflict. In the two-command fixture, the head conflicts while the later edit targets the current revision. Resolution is rejected, the complete queue and its file bytes stay unchanged, and FIFO replay produces no later effect. An injected failed write also preserves the original conflict. These checks establish the bounded local policy; a supplied current revision can still become stale before a real server receives the replacement.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the delete/recreate answer, the original create uses revision zero, the accepted delete uses revision one, and an explicit recreate uses revision two because the synthetic tombstone still carries the newer revision. The recreate is a new user intent with a fresh operation ID. Replay the queued delete before recreating, or define the dependency as a single ordered lane. Assert the server value is nil after delete acknowledgment and only becomes the chosen new title after the new command is accepted.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use the manual acknowledgment seam for the account schedule: capture A's ticket, send the command to the synthetic server, activate B, activate A again, and call record with the old ticket. staleAccount is the expected local result. Fetch B through its own current activation and verify its records remain unrelated. Then reactivate A, replay its retained pending command, and resolve the existing server receipt without another effect. This proves ownership at the local commit boundary rather than pretending the first service effect disappeared.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Finally, preserve the original before-image file for an injected RejectingDisk test. Both the bytes and a same-actor read must still reflect the acknowledged state after failure. Keep the ordinary restart proof and these injected failure proofs in separate evidence rows. A crash during rename, actual disk exhaustion, scheduler grants and receipt-retention negotiation remain further verification, with no automatic learner completion.\u003c/p>"
      },
