        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A weak reference reaches an object without keeping it alive. But \u003ccode>guard let self\u003c/code> creates a strong local reference for the outer callback's body. An inner stored closure can capture that reference strongly. Draw screen → queue → inner closure → screen: a new retain cycle is possible. Writing weak on the outer closure does not make the inner closure's reference weak.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Decide whether the queued operation belongs to the screen. If its only purpose is updating a visible screen, give the inner callback its own weak capture and define removal or cancellation when the flow ends. If a save must finish after dismissal, a dedicated operation owner can retain the service and data it needs, while treating screen updates separately. Strong retention until a reliable completion and release path is bounded ownership; work queued indefinitely lacks that assurance. Inspect completion, cancellation, and queue clearing, then use a controlled pending callback to test release. Replacing weak with unowned would substitute a lifetime assumption and possible trap for an ownership policy.\u003c/p>",
