        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The first call checks that the weak owner still exists, then asks the service for names. Empty success becomes loaded with an empty list and displays no matches. Offline takes the error path and displays retry. Neither outcome has names to display, but they mean different things. An exhaustive summary, which handles every state case, keeps their next actions separate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The saved variable strongly owns the closure, but the closure's weak capture does not own ReviewOwner. Once every other strong owner is removed, the weak probe can become nil. Calling the saved closure then reaches its absent-owner guard and returns safely. The local self created by successful unwrapping temporarily owns the instance during use. Because the initializer constructs a stored closure directly, there is no incoming plain closure parameter requiring an escaping annotation. Finally, ownership safety does not establish lifecycle correctness: an owner retained elsewhere may still be alive after its flow ends, so asynchronous extensions would need an explicit update and cancellation policy.\u003c/p>",
