        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>\u003ccode>@State\u003c/code> connects the root's locally owned draft to storage for a stable SwiftUI identity. Identity is how SwiftUI recognizes the same place in the interface across new view descriptions. \u003ccode>@Binding\u003c/code> gives a child read and write access to another owner's value. \u003ccode>@Bindable\u003c/code> supplies editing bindings such as \u003ccode>$draft.name\u003c/code> for an observable model; it does not create a second draft.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A read-only child can receive a plain model property and read name or computed canContinue in its body. Observation tracks those property dependencies; it does not require that child to own the model. \u003ccode>@MainActor\u003c/code> separately controls where those properties may be changed. Neither annotation persists the draft, restores a process, or defines logout cleanup. Keeping identity stable preserves local state, while intentionally replacing identity can reset it. These examples require supported Observation integration and do not replace the older ObservableObject wrappers used by other lesson examples.\u003c/p>",
