        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>State takes its initial value when SwiftUI creates storage for an identity. A new initializer argument does not necessarily replace existing storage at that same identity. Thus A's unsaved draft can remain after the parent supplies B. This can protect edits, but it conflicts with an editor meant to switch records. Decide who owns replacement and what happens to unsaved work.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Choose an explicit contract: have the parent own the active draft, implement a replace-record operation with unsaved-edit handling, or use a stable record-based identity when a record switch should create a fresh editor. A random UUID on every render repeatedly destroys local state and explains the erased typing. Test that an unrelated parent change preserves edits, that switching records follows the chosen confirmation or reset rule, and that explicit Reset behaves independently. Keep model initialization free of required side effects because description construction can create candidate instances without replacing existing storage. Persistence remains a separate requirement.\u003c/p>",
