        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>MainActor protects access to the model's UI state, but waiting lets other work run. While A is suspended at \u003ccode>await\u003c/code>, B can become the current request. Give each request a revision, a value identifying which request is current. Capture it before waiting. After the service returns, check both cancellation and the revision before publishing loaded, even if the service ignored cancellation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Perform the same freshness comparison in the catch path. Otherwise A's late error or cancellation can replace B's newer loaded result with failed or idle. Only the current request may apply the model's cancellation policy; this example chooses idle, while another feature could retain earlier content. Classify cancellation using the task state and the service's documented cancellation behavior rather than assuming every API throws the same error type. The async keyword does not guarantee off-main work, and actor isolation does not preserve pre-await assumptions. Use controlled A/B completions to verify both late success and late failure.\u003c/p>",
