        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The struct copies its field values, but its notes field still points to a mutable object. \u003ccode>let\u003c/code> fixes which object is referenced; it does not freeze the object's text. Use an immutable snapshot made of safely transferable values, or let an actor own and control mutable notes. Actor references can cross isolation boundaries safely because access to isolated members still follows the actor's rules.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Sendable\u003c/code> promises safe transfer of values. \u003ccode>@Sendable\u003c/code> constrains closure captures, the values a closure remembers, and their use. \u003ccode>@unchecked Sendable\u003c/code> is a manual promise and adds no lock, so it needs a complete documented protection mechanism that the compiler cannot establish. Marking an accessor nonisolated opts it out of actor isolation; it cannot thereby safely read arbitrary mutable isolated state. Return a snapshot through an isolated operation instead. Likewise, referencing a MainActor model from another domain does not permit synchronous mutation of its UI properties. Diagnose the ownership and access invariant rather than suppressing the diagnostic.\u003c/p>",
