        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Ending normally on failure erases the error. A best-effort notification source may allow that, but a data reader expecting failure loses its recovery signal. Use a throwing sequence when errors are part of the promise, or document the deliberate change. Test failure separately from normal completion. A bridge is equivalent only when callers observe the intended behavior, not merely a similar-looking return type.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The reverse adapter starts work before subscription, so a nonreplaying subject can miss early values. It also lacks a defined stop path if subscriber cancellation never reaches the producing task. Choose subscription-driven start or deliberate replay according to the source, and assign ownership of the producer handle. For the async side, connect termination to cleanup and provide an explicit stop when an early consumer exit otherwise retains the source. Verify creation without consumption, late subscription, early stop, and completion races with controlled fixtures. These are adapter risks supported by their structure; whether any caller experiences harm still requires tracing its assumptions and observing the relevant path.\u003c/p>",
