        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Choose storage by what losing the data means. UserDefaults fits small ordinary preferences. Cache storage fits data that can be fetched again after purging. The only copy of a user draft needs durable storage, meaning it survives the process, plus schema, atomic replacement (readers see a complete file) or transactions, recovery, protection, and backup rules. Put credentials behind a Keychain interface with explicit item identity and an access policy.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The actor fake's account-keyed dictionary demonstrates isolated in-memory replacement and separation of account A from B. Async protocol requirements allow callers to enter actor isolation, and synchronous isolated operations can perform replacement without suspension halfway through the change. Constructor injection makes its lifetime visible. These properties do not establish persistence across termination, database migration, protected-data behavior, or Keychain access. A disk adapter must preserve its framework's queue or context confinement even when called through a repository actor. Test actual adapter failures separately, and connect logout clearing to pending-load invalidation so old work cannot refill cleared account data.\u003c/p>",
