        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Name the owners first: composition chooses the implementation; the account service owns credentials; verification and rollout have accountable owners too. Route snapshotting gives each request one dispatcher for its lifetime. Drain-then-promote waits for active requests before switching. Compare complexity and how long shared sessions must survive. Begin with one read operation, preserving status, cancellation, headers, cookies, trust, interception, and tracing through injected fixtures.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Require parity tests and controlled in-flight switching before a bounded rollout. Agree on outcome, duration, cancellation, refresh, and active-request measures with approved aggregate fields. Set gates for each cohort, a comparable user or device group. Define stop conditions against matched baselines and mark missing evidence pending. Recovery selects the old dispatcher for future requests according to the chosen switching policy. It must not automatically replay a timed-out write through another transport, because that can duplicate server effects. Name who pauses expansion and how retained old resources are released. After successful adoption and stable evidence, retire the old path and temporary switching machinery with owner review. If the experiment is rejected, remove the candidate alternate path instead; a source review alone establishes no deployed migration.\u003c/p>",
