        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A signing identity needs both the certificate and its matching private key. A provisioning profile authorizes a combination of identity, app, capabilities, and distribution conditions. Entitlements request capabilities and must agree with that authorization and account setup. A certificate alone cannot sign. Diagnose the specific mismatch: bundle identity, missing key, expired profile, or unsupported entitlement. Also check applicable device authorization rather than assuming simulator success covers it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Record the source manifest and actual build route rather than editing a generated project as the durable input. The ledger should connect commit, dependency pins, toolchain, scheme, configuration, environment, archive, version, build, test result bundles, and each binary's matching symbols. A marketing version cannot replace UUIDs, identifiers for the exact compiled binaries. Inspect actual artifacts and signing outputs: a green simulator run does not prove distribution installation or archived resource inclusion. Simulator test hosts may still need the expected signing and Keychain context, so disabling signing universally is unsafe as a diagnostic shortcut. Keep clean-install and upgrade evidence separate when validating the candidate release.\u003c/p>",
