        "scenario": "SyntheticSlot has id: String and title: String. Input is a/First, b/Second, a/Replacement. Return a/First, b/Second without changing input. IDs are case-sensitive. The mapper needs no UI isolation.",
        "androidBridge": "Kotlin filtering with a mutable membership set expresses the same algorithmic boundary. Swift structs and arrays have value semantics, while Set iteration does not promise input order; a protocol adapter is a replacement seam rather than a requirement for a particular Android or iOS DI framework.",
        "rubric": [
          "Uses a membership set while traversing the original input.",
          "Keeps each first complete slot, including its title.",
          "Tests conflicting duplicate titles, empty, all-duplicate, distinct, and case-sensitive inputs.",
          "Keeps mapping pure and separates expected algorithmic work from measured speed."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Walk the original list in order. Keep a set of IDs already seen, and keep a slot only when its ID is new. Thus a/First is retained, b/Second follows, and a/Replacement is discarded. The set answers whether an ID occurred; it does not supply output order or titles. This pure mapper changes no external state and returns the original first complete values:\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">struct SyntheticSlot: Equatable {\n    let id: String\n    let title: String\n}\nfunc mapSlots(_ input: [SyntheticSlot]) -&gt; [SyntheticSlot] {\n    var seen = Set&lt;String&gt;()\n    return input.filter { seen.insert($0.id).inserted }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The input fixture returns a/First followed by b/Second. Comparing only IDs would miss accidentally retaining Replacement. Assert complete output values, plus empty input, all duplicates, all distinct, and IDs differing only in case. The contract deliberately introduces no case folding. Keep this pure implementation behind the bounded mapping seam so the old implementation can be compared using synthetic fixtures without duplicating side effects. A pure mapper needs no MainActor annotation merely because another module protocol has one. Set membership adds storage and changes expected work; representative CPU, memory, and screen measurements are still required before claiming an optimization outcome.\u003c/p>",
