        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A generic parameter gives a type a name and keeps a relationship visible: identity’s T input returns T without a cast, and Source’s associated Element can remain visible through a constrained generic. Uppercase Any instead accepts unrestricted values with little declared capability. Lowercase any Protocol is an existential: a value used through its protocol capabilities while its concrete type is hidden. That concrete type may be a struct or a class.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Result-position some Protocol hides the name of one chosen implementation type. Think of a factory that always makes the same kind of tool without revealing its brand. Ordinary branches cannot return unrelated concrete conformers merely because both satisfy the protocol. Parameter-position some is generic shorthand; independent some parameters do not imply one shared type. A constrained existential can still specify an associated type, such as Element == Int, so any does not always discard every relationship. Uppercase Self refers to the current/conforming type, while lowercase self is the current instance. Choose the contract from information the caller needs, then test that boundary before claiming optimization benefits. For GooglePlaceDetailsServing, inheriting Sendable is the type’s transfer-safety contract. Its fetchDetails method is async throws: it may suspend and fail. Those method effects do not supply Sendable conformance or promise background execution.\u003c/p>",
