        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A Timer reaching its due time is like an alarm ready to ring. The RunLoop must also be checking a mode that includes that timer and get a chance to process it. UIKit interaction can use tracking behavior that excludes default-only registration. Registering for common modes makes the timer eligible in the modes designated common for that loop; it does not include every custom mode, create a new thread, or guarantee punctual delivery.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Check how the Timer was created and which modes it was registered in. A busy main-thread callback or invalidation can also explain missing updates, so correlate callback samples with interaction and thread activity. A countdown should calculate from its chosen clock and deadline rather than count ticks, since missed firings need not be replayed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Choose whether this feature should update during interaction, then keep its callback small and name when its owner invalidates it on the same thread. Invalidation ends the timer’s registration. Handler posting is a useful thread-loop analogy, but copying that mental model without mode membership misses the iOS mechanism. The host custom-mode probe cannot establish UIKit scrolling behavior.\u003c/p>",
