        "androidBridge": "Compose measurement and placeables help describe geometry. SwiftUI proposals are not hard constraints, and layout cache callbacks do not imply Compose measurement-cache semantics.",
        "rubric": [
          "Checks current dimensions, baselines, row-break key, width and gaps before reusing this logical arrangement.",
          "Separates updateCache from recomputing for a changed proposal.",
          "Calculates ascent 20, descent 10 and wide-row y offsets 5 and 0.",
          "Calculates narrow height 62 and uses consistent measurement and placement proposals.",
          "Passes logical x unchanged because SwiftUI flips RTL placement and must not be made to reflect it twice.",
          "Keeps logical IDs and states container-baseline and overflow limits."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Two children can stay two children while their sizes or row positions change. Width, font, measured dimensions, baseline guides, row-break keys and spacing all affect this arrangement. Clear the cache in updateCache, but also compare the relevant measured inputs for the current proposal on every arrangement request. That set of inputs is the cache fingerprint. The lab remeasures current proxy dimensions and caches only the pure arrangement, so it makes no unsupported promise to eliminate text measurement cost.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Call the first child A and the second B. At width 120 they fit with an eight-point gap. Ascent is the height above the baseline: max(15,20) = 20. Descent is the height below it: max(5,10) = 10. Place A at (0,5) and B at (48,0) so both baselines reach 20; the row height is 30. At width 70 they wrap: A is at (0,0), B is at (0,32), and total height is 20 + 12 + 30 = 62. Placement should use the same capped-width, unspecified-height measurement policy, with bounds origin added afterward.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For each given width, keep those logical positions in both left-to-right and right-to-left layout. SwiftUI owns the direction change when placing subviews. SwiftUI automatically flips subview x placement in RTL; manually reflecting first would double-flip it. A numeric UnitPoint anchor does not disable that behavior. Keep logical order and IDs, and verify native RTL separately. Oversize children follow the stated overflow policy. Internal row baseline alignment does not establish an explicit container first/last baseline contract; that needs separate implementation and typography tests.\u003c/p>",
