        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Choose one owner for vertical scrolling. Let the choices scroll horizontally unless a second vertical scroll area is essential. The two directions then have separate jobs. Leave native arbitration intact before adding custom gestures. A broad zero-distance or high-priority drag can steal page scrolling and still fail after cancellation. If same-axis nesting is necessary, specify its viewport, handoff, and accessible alternatives rather than translating Compose nestedScroll callbacks literally.\u003c/p>\u003cp>FocusState tracks which field receives editing input. Use one optional Field enum with a distinct case for each TextField. AccessibilityFocusState separately requests assistive focus, such as on an error element. On submit, advance to the intended field and request scrolling through the current ScrollViewReader proxy and stable field ID. Validation can clear editing focus and separately request assistive focus on an installed error element. That does not guarantee a software keyboard, announcement, or hardware Tab order.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If content still overflows, compare the custom container with a simpler stacked form. Assign a feature owner for focus/validation and a container owner for layout/arbitration. Verify diagonal drags, software submit/toolbar progression, Tab and Shift-Tab, VoiceOver reading/activation, large text, and RTL on named destinations. Keep drafts intact during cancellation. Compiler and host geometry checks are useful but do not certify those runtime outcomes.\u003c/p>",
