        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>One running app process can share services across windows. Each scene separately owns its navigation and presentation. UISceneSession identifies a persistent interaction session. A connected UIScene, its windows and its controllers are the live UI objects for that session. scene(_:willConnectTo:options:) can create or restore an interface for an existing session, so it is not a new-session test. Disconnection is also different from permanently discarding that session.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Inactive can mean a temporary interruption. It does not always mean the scene entered the background. Reactivation therefore need not include a background-to-foreground callback. A view's scenePhase reflects its containing scene, while an App-level phase aggregates scenes and can stay active while another window is inactive. Quiet the affected visual work without indiscriminately cancelling a shared account service used elsewhere.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A controller disappearing, a View description being rebuilt, the process launching and a durable draft being deleted are different lifetime events. SwiftUI view descriptions can change while identity-associated state survives. Preserve important drafts through normal durable commits: a suspended process may terminate without applicationWillTerminate. The familiar launch/foreground/active path is illustrative rather than universal. Use WindowGroup for scene roots and one delegate adaptor for needed process-level callbacks, then verify actual routes and transitions on a destination.\u003c/p>",
