        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Hiding the row does not mean its delete has reached the server. Keep the saved delete command. If the server forgets the latest revision too, an old update can look like a new create. The deleted record can then reappear. A tombstone is a retained marker of deletion with the revision needed to reject that old write. I would retain a revision-bearing tombstone or use another explicit server policy that rejects stale intent during the supported offline window.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This bounded fixture processes one account’s commands in first-in, first-out order and pauses when a conflict appears. That sacrifices throughput for reviewable ordering. Per-record lanes are an alternative only after delete/recreate dependencies and concurrent acknowledgments are specified. A revision mismatch is retained for user/domain resolution rather than retried forever.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Deliberately recreating the record or rebasing an edit onto the current revision is new intent: it receives a fresh operation ID and a current precondition. Reusing the conflicted ID with changed payload violates idempotency. I would test delete persistence through reload, stale update rejection and A-to-B-to-A before acknowledgment. The late effect may already exist remotely, but its old ticket cannot commit locally. Tombstone retention, server authorization and cross-device merge remain independently owned contracts.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A new operation ID alone does not preserve the order of dependent intent. Either reconcile the entire record’s dependent command lane and preconditions atomically, or support only one pending command per record and replace it in its original slot. Reject unsupported dependencies without changing the persisted queue.\u003c/p>",
