        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Keep the conservative admission limit while the required responsiveness, memory and energy evidence is missing. Finishing sooner alone is not enough. A shorter batch is useful only if it improves the intended outcome without unacceptable interaction delay, memory peak or sustained pressure. Keep projection, decode, caption and preparation fixed, then compare matched one-worker and four-worker batches on representative hardware. Retain worker durations, actual overlap, batch elapsed, main-thread interaction, pressure history and decoded-image peak separately.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Host checks cover ordered output, corruption, unique-file round trips and cleanup, and cooperative cancellation. They do not measure device energy. A supported power or energy observation must retain actual units, tool version, workload window, display and charging conditions. This local workload has no radio traffic, so radio-saving claims are out of scope. I would name the performance owner and product budget reviewer, predeclare bounded reruns and recover by restoring the previous queue-admission limit while keeping cancellation and file-ownership rules intact. If results remain inconclusive, the proposed policy remains conditional with a specific missing artifact, rather than retrying until a favorable percentile appears or inventing battery savings.\u003c/p>",
        "followUps": [
          "What decision follows if throughput improves but a constrained device’s memory peak becomes unsafe?",
          "What extra energy conditions must be recorded when networking is added?"
        ],
        "pitfalls": [
          "Treating elapsed time as an energy measurement.",
          "Widening the pool based only on the fastest device or one CPU trace."
        ]
