        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The current check was accepted for small local files. A large bundle changes that workload, so revise the UI design without expanding the old evidence claim. I would characterize representative bundle size and responsiveness before claiming a performance defect or improvement. A streaming digest reads chunks instead of holding the whole file. Keep the same declared rules for hashing directory names and file contents. Reduced buffering is a design property; actual memory benefit still needs evidence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Decide explicitly where the work executes: creating a Swift task from main-actor code does not prove blocking file reads moved away from UI-critical execution. The consumer captures candidate identity and a generation, requests cancellation on navigation or replacement, and checks both before committing status. Cooperative cancellation may arrive after a synchronous read finishes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check equal digests for equal inputs, detection of changed files, cancellation checkpoints and rejection of late success and error. Then obtain the actual target interaction and recording evidence. Independent review checks the revised mechanism and limits. Without a device recording, I can report functional host checks and a proposed execution design, not measured responsiveness or production benefit.\u003c/p>",
